> Questions
> 1. Since an unstructured covariance is probably too complex with 4 
> time points, would you prefer a random intercept (compound symmetry) 
> or AR(1)? Would a random slope for time be unnecessary at this sample 
> size?

 ? ?I prefer to include random slopes wherever possible (see Forstmeier and Schielzeth 2009). AR(1) is probably more suited to the example (evenly spaced samples in time). You may still find that this model results in a singular fit ...

> 2. For the percentage and score outcomes: if I have the counts or a 
> fixed number of items, would you use a binomial model with the known 
> denominator (checking for overdispersion), or is a Gaussian linear 
> mixed model acceptable when scores are far from the boundaries? For 
> percentages with some values at 0 or 100, would you prefer binomial or 
> beta?

 ? ? In general I prefer binomial models, as they handle 'perfect' 
(0%/100%) cases in a natural way. If you wanted to use beta you'd either need a zero-one inflated model (ZOIB) or an ordered-beta (`family=ordbeta` in glmmTMB, or the `ordbetareg` package if you're willing to go Bayesian)

> 4. For the Gaussian outcomes, is REML with Kenward-Roger or 
> Satterthwaite degrees of freedom sufficient at 45 clusters?

 ? Yes (Kenward-Roger is generally better for cases where it's computationally feasible).

> For the GLMMs, you suggested Gauss-Hermite quadrature and a parametric 
> bootstrap; would you report the bootstrap for every model, or only for 
> the key tests?

 ? ? Not sure what this means. You should be able to derive the confidence intervals for all the parameters with a single PB run ...

> 5. Could you point me to any articles or books that I could cite for 
> the sample size (number of clusters) needed for linear and generalized 
> linear mixed models, and for the small-sample issues you mentioned? I 
> would like to cite published sources in my study to support the 
> analysis plan.
 ? ? Check
https://stats.stackexchange.com/questions/37647/what-is-the-minimum-recommended-number-of-groups-for-a-random-effects-factor
and
https://bbolker.github.io/mixedmodels-misc/glmmFAQ.html#should-i-treat-factor-xxx-as-fixed-or-random
; there's not much published since it's hard to specify a hard-and-fast limit, but you could probably cite Gelman and Hill or Crawley (both mentioned in the GLMM FAQ, I think). Might also be mentioned in Bolker et al 2009.
